              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.4: Use of muscular force of animals to assist with human tasks"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.326,
            0.352,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ever heard of ..."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.14,
            0.359,
            0.744,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Muscular force plays an important role in many functions inside our body too. This force helps us chew food and push it through the alimentary canal during the process of digestion. The expansion and contraction of our heart muscles allows the blood to circulate in our body — a process essential for survival."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.744,
            0.357,
            0.826,
            0.456
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.744,
                0.357,
                0.826,
                0.456
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.49,
            0.211,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Friction"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.513,
            0.597,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A ball rolling on a flat ground stops on its own after some time. If we stop pedalling our bicycle on a flat road, it slows down and comes to a stop."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.616,
            0.515,
            0.784,
            0.552
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is there any other contact force?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.792,
            0.494,
            0.906,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.792,
                0.494,
                0.906,
                0.603
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.568,
            0.745,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If the road is rough, it stops sooner than on a smoother road. You must have come across many such experiences. What causes the change in the speed of objects in such situations? We have learnt earlier that a force is essential to change the speed of an object. However, in all these situations no force appears to be acting on the objects, yet their speed gradually decreases and they come to a stop after some time. Is it possible that some force is indeed acting on them? Which force is that?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.734,
            0.532,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.3: Let us investigate"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.777,
            0.744,
            0.817
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Take an object with a flat base (such as an empty lunch box/ geometry box/ a notebook) and place it on a table or floor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.82,
            0.744,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Gently push it and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "observe",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 5.5). Does it stop after travelling some distance? Is there a force acting on it which brings it to rest?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.881,
            0.743,
            0.921
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "z Now repeat by pushing the object in the opposite direction. Does it stop again after travelling some distance?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 18,
